{%- comment -%}
  The carousel shell, shared by every section whose row can be one — Most Loved
  and Our Products today. It is deliberately the *shell* and nothing else: the
  caller renders its own cells, because only the caller knows what a card is,
  and hands them over already wrapped.

  The carousel is **not in the design**. Every part of how it moves is, though,
  which is what keeps it reading as the same maison rather than as a plugin
  dropped in — see `.row-carousel` in base.css for where each motion, the
  arrows and the marks come from.

  Nothing here is load-bearing without scripting. The track is the very same
  `.grid-auto` element the plain layout ships, so a visitor with no JavaScript
  gets the grid — every card in it, every link real — and the controls, which
  would be a lie, are rendered `hidden` and unhidden only once the script finds
  more than one page.

  Takes:
    cells        the markup of every cell, each already wrapped in
                 `.row-carousel__cell` with `data-row-cell`
    count        how many marks to render — the most pages there could ever be,
                 which is one card per page, so the number of cells
    motion       `slide` or `fade`
    arrows       `bottom`, `top`, `side` or `header`
    heading      optional heading text; rendered only by the `header`
                 placement, on the left of the band the arrows sit in
    label        the accessible name for the group
    track_class  the grid classes the track carries, e.g. `grid-auto--products`
    track_style  inline custom properties for that grid
    layout_desktop, layout_tablet, layout_mobile
                 `grid` or `carousel`; omitted means carousel at that size

  `header` is a hairline band above the track: the heading on the left, the
  two arrows together on the right, and no marks — the band is the whole
  statement of position, an end saying so by its arrow disabling. The heading
  sits outside `data-row-controls`, so a row with nothing to cycle through
  keeps its title while the arrows go.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign arrows_at = arrows | default: 'bottom'

  comment
    Where the arrows go. Under the track by default, which is what the row
    shipped with; over it; or either side of it, where they take columns of
    their own rather than lying over a card — a card is clickable to its edges
    here, and the hero sets its own arrows beside the cards rather than on them.

    Beside the track the two arrows and the marks are three separate visibility
    units, since no one element wraps them; under or over it they share the
    control row and it is the unit. `data-row-controls` is what the script
    hides when there is nothing to cycle through, so it goes wherever that unit
    happens to be.
  endcomment
  assign side_arrows = false
  if arrows_at == 'side'
    assign side_arrows = true
  endif
-%}

{%- capture arrow_prev -%}
  <button
    class="row-carousel__arrow row-carousel__arrow--prev"
    type="button"
    data-row-step="-1"
    aria-label="{{ 'general.carousel.previous' | t }}"
    {% if side_arrows %}data-row-controls hidden{% endif %}
  >
    {% render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-left' %}
  </button>
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture arrow_next -%}
  <button
    class="row-carousel__arrow row-carousel__arrow--next"
    type="button"
    data-row-step="1"
    aria-label="{{ 'general.carousel.next' | t }}"
    {% if side_arrows %}data-row-controls hidden{% endif %}
  >
    {% render 'icon', icon: 'arrow-right' %}
  </button>
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The marks. The window clips and the track inside it is what moves, so the set
  never wraps onto a second row however many pages there are — the script
  shifts it by whole pixels through `--row-mark-shift` and the marks either
  side of the current one half-show past the edge.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture marks -%}
  <div
    class="row-carousel__dots"
    data-row-dots
    {% if side_arrows %}data-row-controls hidden{% endif %}
  >
    <div class="row-carousel__dots-track" data-row-dots-track>
      {%- for i in (1..count) -%}
        <button
          class="row-carousel__dot"
          type="button"
          data-row-dot="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'general.carousel.go_to_page' | t: number: forloop.index }}"
          hidden
        ></button>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture viewport -%}
  <div class="row-carousel__viewport" data-row-viewport>
    {%- comment -%}
      The ruler. It is never seen and never in the flow — it exists so the
      script can ask the browser how wide a column would be under `.grid-auto`'s
      rule instead of re-deriving that rule in JavaScript. Every threshold, the
      product grid's single-column override included, stays in the stylesheet
      where the grid's own are.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="row-carousel__track grid-auto {{ track_class }}" data-row-track style="{{ track_style | strip }}">
      <div class="row-carousel__ruler" data-row-ruler aria-hidden="true"></div>
      {{ cells }}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="row-carousel reveal"
  data-row-carousel
  data-row-motion="{{ motion | default: 'slide' }}"
  data-row-arrows="{{ arrows_at }}"
  data-row-layout-desktop="{{ layout_desktop | default: 'carousel' }}"
  data-row-layout-tablet="{{ layout_tablet | default: 'carousel' }}"
  data-row-layout-mobile="{{ layout_mobile | default: 'carousel' }}"
  role="group"
  aria-roledescription="{{ 'general.carousel.role' | t }}"
  aria-label="{{ label | escape }}"
>
  {%- if side_arrows -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The frame exists to be laid out, because the carousel around it exists to
      be measured: a container cannot be styled by a query against its own size,
      and what changes on a narrow row here is the arrangement — which lives on
      the container. Same shape, and the same reason, as `.promises` and
      `.footer__cols`.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="row-carousel__frame">
      {{ arrow_prev }}
      {{ viewport }}
      {{ arrow_next }}
      {{ marks }}
    </div>
  {%- elsif arrows_at == 'header' -%}
    <div class="row-carousel__header">
      {%- if heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="row-carousel__heading">{{ heading }}</h2>
      {%- endif -%}
      <div class="row-carousel__header-arrows" data-row-controls hidden>
        {{ arrow_prev }}{{ arrow_next }}
      </div>
    </div>
    {{ viewport }}
  {%- elsif arrows_at == 'top' -%}
    <div class="row-carousel__controls" data-row-controls hidden>
      {{ arrow_prev }}{{ marks }}{{ arrow_next }}
    </div>
    {{ viewport }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{ viewport }}
    <div class="row-carousel__controls" data-row-controls hidden>
      {{ arrow_prev }}{{ marks }}{{ arrow_next }}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
